-- title:
--   Divisibility of the class group J₀(N) over ℚ̄
-- statement:
--   Let $N$ be a natural number with $N \neq 0$ (supplied by the instance `NeZero N`). Write `JZero N` for the project's abbreviation `Pic0 (AlgebraicClosure ℚ) (modularFunctionFieldBar N)`, that is, the group of degree-zero divisor classes of the function field `modularFunctionFieldBar N` over its constant field $\bar{\mathbb Q}$, where `modularFunctionFieldBar N` is `laurentBaseChange (AlgebraicClosure ℚ) (modularFunctionFieldFull N)`: the subfield of Laurent series over $\bar{\mathbb Q}$ generated by the constants and by the coefficientwise images of the project's full modular function field of level $N$ inside `LaurentSeries ℚ`. The theorem asserts that this additive group is divisible, in the explicit unfolded form: for every natural number $m$ with $m \neq 0$ and every element $x$ of `JZero N` there exists an element $y$ of `JZero N` with $m \bullet y = x$, the scalar action being the natural-number multiple in the additive group. No hypotheses beyond $N \neq 0$ and the ambient typeclass instances appear; in particular the multiplier ranges over natural numbers rather than integers, divisibility is stated pointwise (an existence statement for each $x$) rather than as a `DivisibleBy`-type instance, and nothing is asserted about uniqueness of $y$ or about the structure of the $m$-torsion.
--
--   Classically this is the statement that the Jacobian of a smooth projective curve over an algebraically closed field of characteristic zero is a divisible group, applied to $J_0(N)$ over $\bar{\mathbb Q}$; see Mumford's and Milne's accounts of abelian varieties. The formal statement is phrased entirely in terms of the project's divisor-theoretic model: `JZero N` is the degree-zero divisor class group of the base-changed modular function field, not an abelian variety, and divisibility is asserted only for natural multipliers. It is used downstream where one must divide classes or control indices of multiplication maps, for instance in [`ModularCurve.JZero.finiteIndex_range_nsmul_two_invariants`](thm.html#ModularCurve.JZero.finiteIndex_range_nsmul_two_invariants) on the index of $2\cdot(\text{Galois invariants})$, and in the reduction and specialisation machinery for Hecke correspondences on the modular curve.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_ModularCurve_JZero_divisible.lean

import Definitions.Def_ModularCurve_ArithmeticGalois

set_option maxHeartbeats 4000000
set_option synthInstance.maxHeartbeats 400000
